  "image_name": "2bef79ee48aaa2c8e834cd53388eaf3d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how saline groundwater can accumulate and be extracted in an arid basin. In the upper block diagram, a low-lying basin receives intermittent runoff from ephemeral rivers descending from surrounding highlands. Sediment from these streams forms alluvial fans around the basin margins, while a shallow central water body loses water mainly through strong evaporation. Some surface water also infiltrates downward, and faults or fractures in the basement appear to provide deeper pathways for groundwater movement.\n\nThe lower cross-section summarizes the subsurface arrangement that controls fresh versus briny water. Different sedimentary facies are layered across the basin: more permeable evaporitic and sandstone units are interbedded with less permeable claystone and conglomeratic layers. Blue arrows show fresher groundwater moving from the basin edges inward, while green arrows indicate denser brine circulating and concentrating near the basin center beneath the phreatic level. The red water-table line dips toward the middle, where brine wells tap the central saline reservoir. Overall, the figure conveys a basin-scale hydrologic system in which surface inflow, evaporation, infiltration, and layered permeability combine to focus and preserve brine in the center while fresher water remains more peripheral.",
